Garbage collection happens automatically in JavaScript. Unlike low-level languages such as C or C++ where developers must manually allocate and free memory, 
JavaScript engines handle memory management behind the scenes. 

How Automatic Garbage Collection Works
The process is managed by a background process called the garbage collector, which primarily uses the mark-and-sweep algorithm. 

Mark Phase: 
    The garbage collector starts from "roots" (like global variables and variables in the current function scope) and traces all objects that are reachable or 
    accessible by the program, marking them as "in use".
Sweep Phase: 
    The collector then scans all of memory and reclaims the space occupied by any unmarked objects, which are considered no longer needed. 
    Modern JavaScript engines, such as V8 (used in Chrome and Node.js), use further optimizations like generational garbage collection, which categorizes 
    objects by age to improve efficiency, but the core principle of automatic management remains the same. 
